Given an ellipse C: $$\frac {x^{2}}{a^{2}}$$+ $$\frac {y^{2}}{b^{2}}$$\=1 (a>b>0) with an eccentricity of $$\frac {1}{2}$$, the area of the quadrilateral formed by the four vertices of ellipse C is 4 $$\sqrt {3}$$.
(1) Find the equation of ellipse C;
(2) Line l intersects ellipse C at two distinct points P($$x_{1}$$,$$y_{1}$$) and Q($$x_{2}$$,$$y_{2}$$), with O as the coordinate origin. If the area of △OPQ is $$\sqrt {3}$$, prove that $$x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}$$ is a constant value.

Given an ellipse $C: \frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+ \frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1 (a > b > 0)$ with left and right foci $F_{1}$ and $F_{2}$, respectively, and an eccentricity of $\frac{1}{2}$. There is a point $M(-3,0)$ such that $\overrightarrow{MF_{2}}=2 \overrightarrow{MF_{1}}$.
