Sarah, Bob, and Alice paint a very long picket fence.
Sarah starts with the first picket and paints every $s$ th picket;
Bob starts with the second picket and paints every $b$ th picket;
Alice starts with the fourth picket and paints every $a$ th picket.
Call the positive integer $1000s+100b+10a$ paintable when the triple $(s,b,a)$ of positive integers results in every picket being painted exactly once by only one of them. Find the sum of all paintable integers.

A certain department store's experience in selling a type of commodity shows that the daily sales volume $y$ (in kilograms) is related to the selling price $x$ (in yuan per kilogram) by the equation: $y = \frac{a}{x-3} + 10(x-6)^2$, where $3 < x < 6$ and $a$ is a constant. When the selling price is 5 yuan per kilogram, the store can sell 11 kilograms of the product per day.
(1) Find the value of $a$;
(2) If the cost of the product is 3 yuan per kilogram, determine the selling price $x$ that maximizes the store's daily profit from selling the product, and find the maximum profit.

Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be positive real numbers satisfying the system of equations:
\begin{align*} \sqrt{2x-xy} + \sqrt{2y-xy} &= 1 \\ \sqrt{2y-yz} + \sqrt{2z-yz} &= \sqrt2 \\ \sqrt{2z-zx} + \sqrt{2x-zx} &= \sqrt3. \end{align*} 
Then $\left[ (1-x)(1-y)(1-z) \right]^2$ can be written as $\frac{m}{n},$ where $m$ and $n$ are relatively prime positive integers. Find $m+n.$

A beverage company has hired a new employee and is conducting a test to determine their salary level. The company has prepared 8 cups of two different beverages, all of the same color, with 4 cups of beverage A and 4 cups of beverage B. The employee is required to taste each cup and select 4 cups of beverage A. If all 4 selections are correct, the monthly salary will be 3500 yuan; if 3 out of 4 selections are correct, the monthly salary will be 2800 yuan, otherwise, the monthly salary will be 2100 yuan. Let X represent the number of correct selections of beverage A by the employee. Assume that the employee has no ability to distinguish between beverages A and B.
(1) Find the probability distribution of X;
(2) Find the expected monthly salary of this employee.

